00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.850
So, you are on Broad City
and it was a comedy duo.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.570
So how what that breaking off,

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:38.514
you been doing that
show since 2009 I think.

00:00:38.514 --> 00:00:39.770
It's been a part of your life.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.340
And that's a long to work with someone

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.670
and all of a sudden
you're like on your own.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.330
- It's crazy to look
back, I'm 32 and like,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.060
10 years is pretty much
a third of my life.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.080
And my partner Abbi Jacobson and I,

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.771
give it up for Abbi, woo!

00:00:52.771 --> 00:00:54.480
(applause)

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:55.313
Abbi!

00:00:56.592 --> 00:00:59.460
Yeah Abbi and I started
the web series in 2009

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.533
and it's just been this
climb and climb to the show.

00:01:02.533 --> 00:01:05.560
We had five seasons, 50
episodes on Comedy Central

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.260
and we created it together and show-ran it

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:11.840
and produced it and we're
so proud of the show

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.170
and it's my favorite show.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.850
- And it's kinda cool
because it didn't end

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.300
because of anyone else, it ended

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.300
because y'all wanted it
to end, which is rare.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.820
- Thank you, Kelly Clarkson yes, I get it.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.510
- I'm just saying that's rare,

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:24.420
usually people are like, "No, more".

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.740
And you're just like, "We're done".

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.170
- And also women don't usually

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.350
get to be like, enough!

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:31.183
In all the ways.

00:01:32.646 --> 00:01:35.150
- I do, you should be in my life.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:37.330
- I should hang out with you more.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.110
This was, I think the first
time, Abbi and I both,

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:39.943
were like, you know what,

00:01:39.943 --> 00:01:41.320
this is a good time to end it

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.180
and it keeps that power

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.290
in the show that we already have created.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.427
- Yeah, so what were you like as a kid?

00:01:47.427 --> 00:01:49.110
Were you both talkative?

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.980
'Cause you both, even
though you might not be

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.800
a standup comedian, you're
a very comedic actor.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.410
Like so were y'all, and
you're very friendly,

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:55.820
we're y'all both like this as kids?

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:56.810
- Where'd you grow up?

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:57.643
- Pittsburgh.

00:01:57.643 --> 00:01:58.476
- Pittsburgh.
- Oh gotchu.

00:01:58.476 --> 00:01:59.309
- You?
- Long Island.

00:01:59.309 --> 00:02:00.870
- Long Island.
- Everyone cool

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.570
is from Long Island!

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:03.750
- Really?
- That's cool.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:04.795
- Yeah I didn't know that.

00:02:04.795 --> 00:02:05.628
- Yeah like Long Island, New Jersey,

00:02:05.628 --> 00:02:06.461
that whole area.

00:02:06.461 --> 00:02:07.460
But were you always this talkative?

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.390
Or were you introverts when you were kids?

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.455
- I was talkative, my brother and I,

00:02:11.455 --> 00:02:13.260
my brother Elliot is also a comedian,

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.260
and we would make sketch
videos for hours, and hours.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.310
I've always been a comedy
nerd, and that's it.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:22.291
- That's awesome.

00:02:22.291 --> 00:02:23.249
- (laughs) That's all I am.

00:02:23.249 --> 00:02:24.082
- Were you always this talkative,

00:02:24.082 --> 00:02:24.915
you knew everyone right off the bat?

00:02:24.915 --> 00:02:25.840
- No, No.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.320
I was very, I was sort of, I needed to be

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.850
a good boy in school, I made good grades.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.260
I was kinda, properly--

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.093
- [Kelly] Strict household?

00:02:35.093 --> 00:02:36.167
- Huh?
- You said good little boy,

00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:37.867
was it a strict household?

00:02:37.867 --> 00:02:38.850
(Ilana laughs)

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:39.683
- They were kind of strict,

00:02:39.683 --> 00:02:40.880
there was some kind of,

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.250
serious demand that we be
well behaved in one area

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:46.720
or another, and school certainly.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.250
So I was kinda constrained.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:50.220
So it was a kind of an outlet,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.880
when I found myself,
I had to find my voice

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.280
by and by as I'm still doing,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.590
but I remember, I had a talent
for drawing and painting,

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:02.670
and my dad would take us
to this special art class,

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.483
me every week and waiting
outside the Carnegie Mellon,

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:08.231
the Carnegie museum,

00:03:08.231 --> 00:03:11.150
when he hadn't shown up yet,
and I would do this thing,

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:12.250
that I can't believe I did,

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.440
I was like 11, or 12, I would see people,

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.380
and then pretend to know them.

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.318
To go, "Oh my gosh, look who it is!".

00:03:21.318 --> 00:03:22.151
(laughter)

00:03:22.151 --> 00:03:23.140
And I would--
- I think there's a word for

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.200
that, it's called stalker.
- That is so strange.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.040
- It's strange, it's strange isn't it?

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.310
- You were 14?

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:29.700
- Yeah, 14 or 11, or 12, or something.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:30.870
- What a bizarre level of agency.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:32.564
To grown-ups, to adults.

00:03:32.564 --> 00:03:33.397
- Do you think it was to make you feel

00:03:33.397 --> 00:03:35.837
more comfortable that you
were alone in that moment?

00:03:35.837 --> 00:03:36.670
- I don't know.
- Wow someone goes to therapy.

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:38.083
- I think it was me--

00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:39.644
- Okay, okay I see you Kelly Clarkson.

00:03:39.644 --> 00:03:41.500
- Hashtag truth!

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:45.110
- No it was the seeds
of something creative.

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:46.410
- Yeah that's so interesting.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.110
I was just wondering where
everybody kinda comes from

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:49.548
and their background,

00:03:49.548 --> 00:03:50.970
but tell us about your
upcoming comedy tour!

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:53.720
- My tour in March is a standup tour,

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.320
and I'm going to nine
cities around the country,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.130
and I'm really excited
about getting out the word

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:01.790
to vote this year, it's 2020.

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.340
It's a big year.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.590
(applause)

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:09.670
And I always just want to say,

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.410
I am just starting to understand

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:13.400
what the Census is,
and why it's important.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.140
But that's also important this year,

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:18.370
so at my standup shows, we're gonna have,

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:20.980
we're partnering with
HeadCount to register voters,

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.780
and then I'm staying an
extra night in each city,

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.760
to throw a voter empowerment dance party

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.830
with my political organization generator--

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.080
- That's awesome!
- So we're gonna dance

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.689
for the right to vote.

00:04:31.689 --> 00:04:32.522
(cloud clapping)

00:04:32.522 --> 00:04:34.414
- That's awesome!

00:04:34.414 --> 00:04:35.908
Because here's the thing,

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:38.710
it's vote for who you love,

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.470
vote for what you believe in, but vote!

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.303
That's important!

00:04:41.303 --> 00:04:43.652
And especially, I always
get upset when I have

00:04:43.652 --> 00:04:45.370
female friends that don't.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:46.778
'Cause it's like do you know hard

00:04:46.778 --> 00:04:49.480
other women had to fight
for us to have the chance

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:50.603
to be able to do it?

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:53.770
Whoever you vote for, I don't care,

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:55.660
vote for who you want
to vote for, but vote.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:56.900
- Right, it's the least we can do,

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:59.130
and it's so, it's everything!

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.420
- And growing up in a small town,

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:02.900
and leaving and going to
other parts of the world,

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:04.960
you really realize how lucky we are,

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.440
so I love that you're doing a dance party,

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.490
- Thanks!
- Making it a cool vibe!

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:10.870
- Yeah the whole tour is
called, "Horny for the Polls",

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:11.778
P-O-L-L-S.

00:05:11.778 --> 00:05:13.530
(laughing)

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:15.921
- I love it!
- Yeah it's really, it's fun.

00:05:15.921 --> 00:05:17.900
Love the clout, thanks.
- Well awesome.

